Z||000003||roadwarrior||08-16-2001||03:13 PM||||my best guess would be to check for deposits in the rad or a blown gasket somewhere.||||reg||1
Z||000004||87GTZ34||08-16-2001||04:01 PM||||Take the thermostat out and run it to check flow only.  Putting a lower temp thermostat is a band-aid a most likely won't do anything unless the one you have now is not working correctly.  It does sound like air pocket to me also.  Does your overflow tank have coolant in it?  When I mess with my cooling system I put the rear up on ramps, take the thermostat out and refill/run (1or 2 min)/check until the fluid stays up to the outlet hole on therm housing.  Then I put the thermostat back in (195 deg) and put coolant halfway up in the overflow tank.  <BR>Also I made a homemade pressure tester by buying an extra thermostat housing cap.  Drill a 1/2 inch hole (or a tight fitting hole) in it and get a new tire valve stem (at tire store). Use silicone sealer on valve and hole and put the valve in.  When the silicone is dry and the engine is cool, put that therm housing cap on and use a hand pump until you hear the overflow tank 'belch'.  Then use a tire pressure gauge and check the pressure on the homemade cap.  It should read 15lbs.  This is tried and true so flamers can flame if they choose, but I will stick by this test.  My car has zero leaks.<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 08-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Indiana_resto_guy||08-16-2001||04:11 PM||||Sounds like the system is operating normally. If you don't like it to run that hot, get a low temp switch and thermostat for it.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mark||08-16-2001||06:15 PM||||I doubt that it has anything to do with your electric cooling fan, since driving down the road at 75 MPH will force lots more air thru the rad than the fan can ever pull. In fact, you shouldn't be seeing the temperature climb enough to turn the cooling fan on unless you are idling in traffic.  My best guesses... <P>1) Thermostat is not opening fully (you can test it in a pan of water). <P>2) Radiator pressure cap is not holding pressure (is the overflow tank filling up?)<P>3) Crudded-up radiator (someone else recently posted a message that replacing the radiator cured their overheating problems).<P>4)  Water pump is not pumping (but you just changed it, so that seems unlikely).||||reg||1
Z||000007||86ttops||08-16-2001||06:22 PM||||thanks I think I will try the thermostat first because that will be cheaper.  You are right the fan never used to come on unless in traffic.  And I replaced the radiator cap already to.  ||||reg||1
